WEBVTT

00:12.860 --> 00:16.000
First, fill the cup half
full of solution “A.”

00:16.000 --> 00:19.040
Then mix in solution “B.”

00:19.910 --> 00:21.770
After mixing lightly,
pour immediately…

00:21.770 --> 00:25.970
…into the silicone mold we
made from the original.

00:25.970 --> 00:28.800
In addition to the mold opening, there
are tiny holes to stop air bubbles…

00:28.800 --> 00:31.410
…from forming, thus allowing the liquid
to reach every part of the mold.

00:31.410 --> 00:32.400
And then?

00:33.950 --> 00:35.790
…we wait a bit.

00:50.670 --> 00:55.730
<b>More Otaku no Video</b>

00:56.080 --> 01:02.010
<i>Over the endless wasteland,
I run alone for all I am worth…</i>

01:02.010 --> 01:08.490
<i>…embracing the hope of an</i>
<i>unseen world far away.</i>

01:08.490 --> 01:14.700
<i>The only thing I believe</i>
<i>in is glowing passion.</i>

01:14.700 --> 01:17.330
<i>I will be a raging inferno!</i>

01:17.330 --> 01:28.570
<i>No one will be able to</i>
<i>stop my heart’s beat!</i>

01:28.570 --> 01:34.510
<i>One of these days, I will find</i>
<i>out the truth of love.</i>

01:34.510 --> 01:40.140
<i>Everything in this world</i>
<i>will be mine.</i>

01:40.140 --> 01:45.720
<i>We’re bound together by friendship.</i>
<i>It’s a thick bond.</i>

01:45.720 --> 01:48.630
<i>Don’t get in my way!</i>
<i>(Don’t get in my way!)</i>

01:48.630 --> 01:51.390
<i>I will follow my path!</i>
<i>(I will follow my path!)</i>

01:51.390 --> 01:57.910
<i>Fight! (Fight!) Fight! (Fight!)</i>
<i>Fight! (Fight!) Fight! (Fight!)</i>

01:57.910 --> 02:03.170
<i>Fight, Otaking!</i>

02:28.750 --> 02:30.440
It’s time!

02:36.200 --> 02:37.050
Well?

02:38.510 --> 02:41.720
Well? Using this technique,
from this single mold…

02:41.720 --> 02:45.340
…we can make hundreds, even
thousands of copies.

02:45.340 --> 02:47.750
That means…

02:47.750 --> 02:51.290
…we can become a plastic
model manufacturer!

02:51.290 --> 02:52.760
That’s right, Kubo!

02:52.760 --> 02:55.360
Already, companies like
Inoue Arts and Kaiyoudo…

02:55.360 --> 02:58.190
…are starting to do
business this way.

02:58.770 --> 03:01.090
Since it’s only sold by mail
order and direct sales…

03:01.090 --> 03:04.030
…you can run a sizable business out
of your own one-room apartment.

03:05.010 --> 03:07.330
And since the buyers are all hardcore
fans, by simply reproducing…

03:07.330 --> 03:10.420
…out-of-print kits, we can charge
outrageous prices!

03:11.370 --> 03:13.700
This is what they call the “garage kit!”

03:13.700 --> 03:14.670
Wait!

03:15.780 --> 03:18.890
I would want this business
to run legally.

03:18.890 --> 03:22.080
What?! If we do that, it’ll take
forever to get started!

03:22.080 --> 03:24.380
Now is the time to strike,
and sell garage kits!

03:24.380 --> 03:27.010
Just hold on a minute.
Calm down, Tanaka.

03:27.630 --> 03:30.390
A silly fad like this will be
gone before you know it.

03:30.390 --> 03:32.930
When that happens,
no matter how hard we work,

03:32.930 --> 03:34.690
…in the end, it’ll amount to nothing.

03:35.010 --> 03:37.670
What we should do is start our own
company… We’ll start small…

03:37.670 --> 03:39.430
…and get some licenses.

03:39.430 --> 03:40.940
We’ll make sure our product
is well-packaged…

03:40.940 --> 03:42.600
…and we should sell it to
wholesalers as well.

03:42.600 --> 03:45.270
We may have to make consignment
sales at first, but that’s okay.

03:45.270 --> 03:48.450
We’ll get modeling magazines
to showcase our product…

03:48.450 --> 03:51.220
…and then we’ll make the world
acknowledge the garage kit.

03:51.220 --> 03:54.630
Kubo! With a plan like that,
we can take over the world!

03:54.630 --> 03:56.920
That’s right! The world
will be in our hands!

03:56.920 --> 03:59.460
Our goal is to control the
world of garage kits…

03:59.460 --> 04:02.080
…and become the supreme
leaders of all otaku!

04:02.080 --> 04:05.750
We’ll name the company
”Grand Prix!” Or “GP” for short!

04:44.110 --> 04:46.400
May 22, that same year.

04:47.060 --> 04:49.170
Opening of the first Grand Prix store.

04:49.930 --> 04:54.390
Kubo, this is our new assistant.

04:54.390 --> 04:57.320
Um… I’m Fukuhara.

04:54.450 --> 04:57.550
Fukuhara

04:57.320 --> 04:59.860
I’m pleased to be working with you.

05:00.320 --> 05:02.260
Right… Welcome aboard.

05:02.260 --> 05:05.620
“Like the Second Coming of Dakko-chan:
The Big Garage Kit boom!”

05:05.620 --> 05:07.420
“Garage Kit Dictionary”

05:06.150 --> 05:07.420
“Garage Kit Close-Up!”

05:07.420 --> 05:10.350
August 18, that same year.

05:11.030 --> 05:14.260
Late-night talk show appearance.
”Today’s Garage Kit Boom”

05:15.440 --> 05:17.800
September 25, that same year.

05:18.000 --> 05:20.340
Shinjuku Station, East exit.

05:26.050 --> 05:29.670
“It’s Okay to Laugh! …Forest of Otaku;
Yamori’s Prayer for Easy Delivery;
Kubo Akira; Others”

05:31.220 --> 05:33.210
October 10, that same year.

05:33.610 --> 05:35.940
Korakuen Stadium.

05:37.560 --> 05:40.190
The First Wonder Carnival.

05:42.630 --> 05:44.600
Kubo

05:45.260 --> 05:47.370
Tanaka

05:48.560 --> 05:51.230
Fukuhara

06:01.450 --> 06:09.690
1987/03/07:
Theatrical premiere of
”Royal Space Force
(The Wings of Honneamise).”

06:11.750 --> 06:17.750
Portrait of an Otaku

06:33.740 --> 06:46.180
Sato Hiroshi
(pseudonym)
Born 1968.
Comic magazine
editor.
Years as an otaku: 13.

06:46.180 --> 06:48.690
What is a garage kit?

06:48.690 --> 06:53.490
A garage kit’s a little different
from other otaku hobbies.

06:53.490 --> 06:57.660
The others don’t have a
real creative aspect…

06:57.660 --> 06:59.290
…but garage kits are different.

06:59.290 --> 07:01.600
With garage kits, you can express…

07:01.600 --> 07:05.200
…your own sensibilities and feelings
for a given character…

07:05.200 --> 07:11.610
…in your own particular way.

07:11.610 --> 07:16.550
With other things, you just collect
them, and that’s the end of it…

07:16.550 --> 07:20.950
…but with garage kits, you not only
have to buy and collect them…

07:20.950 --> 07:25.190
…you have to put your heart and soul
into building and maintaining them.

07:25.190 --> 07:25.750
It’s totally different.

07:26.160 --> 07:28.430
As far as the creative aspects
are concerned…

07:27.400 --> 07:36.940
Voice has been altered at the subject’s
request. Please bear with us.

07:28.430 --> 07:32.730
…garage kits and other things are
as different as night and day.

07:32.730 --> 07:35.140
If I’m not satisfied with
what I’ve bought…

07:35.140 --> 07:37.230
…sometimes I end up building
one completely from scratch.

07:37.230 --> 07:39.270
That’s what’s known as “full-scratch.”

07:39.270 --> 07:45.010
Amateur makers will make molds
and sell copies of these.

07:45.010 --> 07:46.980
Um… Well…

07:46.980 --> 07:48.410
Yes, well…

07:48.410 --> 07:51.880
It’s also different in that you’re always
trying to see how far you can go…

07:51.880 --> 07:54.370
…in expressing your feelings
for a given character.

07:54.370 --> 07:56.870
How are they different
from plastic models?

07:56.870 --> 08:02.490
Plastic models are an industrial product,
so there are lots of restrictions.

08:02.490 --> 08:07.360
Garage kits are different…
Oh, how can I put it?

08:07.360 --> 08:10.340
Well… even though they are
both kits to be assembled…

08:10.340 --> 08:16.740
…with garage kits, you try to put your
heart and soul into the construction.

08:16.740 --> 08:19.310
For that reason, there are
lots of character kits.

08:19.310 --> 08:23.940
When it comes to girl characters, I put
more effort into the ones I like best…

08:23.940 --> 08:28.320
…and into the parts that
I like best as well.

08:28.320 --> 08:30.830
Can you build a model
any way you like?

08:30.830 --> 08:34.190
Eh… If I’m not happy with
a store-bought kit…

08:34.190 --> 08:39.100
…well, chances are I’ll trash
it, leaving only the body…

08:39.100 --> 08:44.380
…and build the rest from scratch.

08:44.770 --> 08:46.910
You can buy something
like this at any store…

08:46.910 --> 08:49.030
…but I can fool with it
a little, remodel it…

08:49.030 --> 08:51.670
…and make it more like
the kind of girl I’d like.

08:51.670 --> 08:58.430
In other words, this doll is the
kind of girl I currently like.

08:58.430 --> 09:00.530
Is it fun?

09:00.530 --> 09:02.380
Yes, it’s fun.

09:02.380 --> 09:06.590
Sooner or later, I think people will
stop buying kits in stores…

09:06.590 --> 09:11.790
…and start building everything from
scratch, all by themselves…

09:11.790 --> 09:14.190
Yeah, just like I’m doing.

09:15.940 --> 09:20.370
An exclusive girlfriend…
The perfect girlfriend…

09:20.370 --> 09:25.240
But she’s just a silent,
cold-skinned doll.

09:25.240 --> 09:29.550
This man bears the burden of the sad
contradiction that is the garage kit.

09:29.550 --> 09:36.390
Just what does the public think
about these garage kits?

09:37.130 --> 09:40.420
As you can see, garage kits
are not yet widely known.

09:40.420 --> 09:44.430
It has not yet achieved the same status
as more mainstream hobbies.

09:44.430 --> 09:49.530
However, as a method of expressing
one’s devotion to the fullest…

09:49.530 --> 09:54.440
…and as an ultimate hobby demanding
a high level of technical skill…

09:54.440 --> 10:00.840
…it is not an exaggeration to say that it
will probably catch on in the future.

10:11.500 --> 10:13.500
GP Head Office Building

10:15.460 --> 10:18.000
President’s Office

10:15.760 --> 10:20.470
I can’t believe it… that we’ve come
this far in just one year!

10:20.500 --> 10:23.000
Representative Director

10:22.240 --> 10:26.310
Tanaka… we could’ve never made
it this far without you.

10:26.300 --> 10:29.680
Managing Director

10:26.310 --> 10:30.240
Kubo, you’ve become the
otaku conqueror…

10:30.240 --> 10:33.840
…the otaku of otaku! Otaking!

10:33.840 --> 10:38.350
No, not yet. That title I can’t
yet claim for myself.

10:38.350 --> 10:40.610
Huh? Why not, Kubo?

10:41.460 --> 10:43.030
I have a dream.

10:43.030 --> 10:45.080
A… A dream?

10:45.080 --> 10:52.460
A utopia of otaku, by otaku, for otaku.
Yes! The building of Otakuland!

10:52.460 --> 10:54.950
“O-Otakuland?!”

10:55.440 --> 11:00.300
Yes. An amusement park that caters
to the needs of any kind of otaku.

11:00.300 --> 11:02.110
That is Otakuland!

11:03.380 --> 11:06.570
There, otaku from all around
the globe can gather…

11:06.570 --> 11:10.880
…and, for a time, forget about reality,
and have fun in the world of otaku.

11:13.050 --> 11:17.400
And then, I’ll put into action my project
for the otakunization of all mankind…

11:17.400 --> 11:22.030
…and make Ueno Yoshiko
regret ever leaving me!

11:22.860 --> 11:25.130
K-Kubo…

11:26.630 --> 11:29.960
I had no idea you were
so unforgiving!

11:29.960 --> 11:31.600
Let’s do it, Tanaka!

11:31.600 --> 11:34.070
We’ll bring to life the dreams
of otaku across the world!

11:34.070 --> 11:36.100
Uh… Uh, yeah!

11:40.810 --> 11:44.520
As you can see, the mail-order division
is doing well, as usual…

11:42.260 --> 11:44.760
GP Headquarters

11:44.520 --> 11:47.770
…with profits up 25% from last year…

11:45.260 --> 11:47.600
Conference Room

11:47.770 --> 11:50.290
…but the direct sales outlets’ profits
have been declining…

11:47.800 --> 11:50.290
Business Manager Iiyama

11:50.290 --> 11:53.620
…since opening the 20 new stores
in the first half of the year…

11:53.620 --> 11:57.690
…and we can see no signs of recovery.

11:57.690 --> 12:00.270
All the wholesalers are complaining
that they’re having a difficult time…

11:59.700 --> 12:02.000
Sato

12:00.270 --> 12:02.740
…due to a lack of advertising.

12:02.150 --> 12:04.900
Advertising Manager Hino

12:02.740 --> 12:05.070
It can’t be a lack of advertising.

12:05.070 --> 12:08.540
We added ten more spots just at
the beginning of this month.

12:08.020 --> 12:10.560
Miyoshi

12:08.540 --> 12:11.000
It’s the effect of the majors
getting into the game.

12:11.000 --> 12:12.370
Especially where licenses
are concerned.

12:12.370 --> 12:14.000
It has become impossible to obtain
licenses from any company…

12:14.000 --> 12:15.550
…under the influence of the majors.

12:15.550 --> 12:17.310
We should check on the
majors’ sales figures.

12:17.310 --> 12:19.910
I’ll bet anything they’re cutting
into our market share.

12:19.910 --> 12:22.750
In other words, there’s a need
to strengthen our efforts…

12:22.750 --> 12:25.350
…in self-licensed and
original products.

12:25.660 --> 12:28.600
If only we had a solid concept…

12:29.200 --> 12:32.230
There’s a project in the works that
has already been submitted…

12:32.230 --> 12:35.130
…to Managing Director Tanaka.

12:35.130 --> 12:36.900
Are you acting on it?

12:36.900 --> 12:41.900
Yeah. We’ve already contracted for
a ¥30 billion bank loan this year.

12:42.170 --> 12:46.180
All right, take it as
far as ¥100 billion.

12:46.180 --> 12:49.410
The new product will be
made entirely in China.

12:49.410 --> 12:50.280
China?!

12:50.280 --> 12:51.420
That’s right.

12:52.790 --> 12:56.020
We’re going to build a major
garage kit factory in China…

12:56.020 --> 12:59.840
…equipped with its own casting,
silicone, and all other facilities.

12:56.760 --> 12:59.090
Heilongjiang

13:01.760 --> 13:03.930
No matter how much
we cut our costs…

13:03.930 --> 13:06.970
…our garage kits are produced
largely by hand…

13:06.970 --> 13:09.220
…and the biggest bottleneck is
therefore the cost of labor.

13:10.070 --> 13:14.670
We must increase our competitiveness
by using abundant Chinese labor.

13:14.670 --> 13:17.470
Or eventually, the majors will devour
our market share by cutting costs.

13:18.010 --> 13:21.150
For our company… No, for the whole
Japanese garage kit industry…

13:21.150 --> 13:27.430
…building a factory in China is a major
undertaking that must be done.

13:27.430 --> 13:31.220
China… Here I come!

14:17.800 --> 14:20.670
Meanwhile, in Japan…

14:29.620 --> 14:32.110
“Garage Kit Depression: The Winter of
the Garage Kits has Arrived at Last”

14:32.110 --> 14:34.790
“GP President in Danger of Dismissal”

14:34.790 --> 14:41.480
“GP Financially Troubled”
”President Hiding Out in China”

14:41.480 --> 14:43.890
“Special Report: The Modeling Industry”

14:43.890 --> 14:47.190
“Director Kirita’s Whole Staff’s
Remaining Hope Fading”

14:47.190 --> 14:51.100
“‘The Potential Power of the Majors
to Threaten Garage Kits… ’

14:51.100 --> 14:53.770
…by Economic Analyst
Ishihara Keiji (Age 35)”

14:55.980 --> 14:57.610
GP Headquarters

14:57.410 --> 15:00.670
Oh! This is incredible!

14:58.100 --> 14:59.750
Development Section

15:00.670 --> 15:03.380
This concept will work
all over the world!

15:03.380 --> 15:06.850
From now on, garage kits have to be
targeted at international markets…

15:06.850 --> 15:08.510
…or there’ll be no point in doing them.

15:08.510 --> 15:13.120
No doubt any modeler will welcome
this character with open arms!

15:13.120 --> 15:14.820
Just who did this design?

15:14.820 --> 15:16.100
This girl did.

15:17.480 --> 15:17.820
Fukuhara

15:17.930 --> 15:22.560
Y-You mean a trendy girl like you
came up with a design like this?

15:24.070 --> 15:25.360
Miss Sato!

15:25.360 --> 15:29.430
If the president’s plan for the
factory in China works out…

15:29.430 --> 15:32.440
…I have an idea for a new
medium to work in.

15:32.440 --> 15:33.080
What?

15:33.080 --> 15:35.480
I’d like to use soft vinyl.

15:35.480 --> 15:36.840
Soft vinyl?

15:36.840 --> 15:39.140
To be sure, soft vinyl does
have disadvantages…

15:39.140 --> 15:41.690
…like changing shape over time,
and being difficult to work with.

15:41.690 --> 15:44.150
But I think that customers
will, from now on…

15:44.150 --> 15:48.390
…want completeness, rather than ease
of alteration, in their models.

15:48.390 --> 15:51.530
With soft vinyl, we can make the
kind of large-scale kits that…

15:51.530 --> 15:53.860
…we couldn’t make with casts.

15:53.860 --> 15:56.660
I also think this can become the
new product that sheds…

15:56.660 --> 15:59.170
…the heavy, bulky image
of garage kits.

15:59.170 --> 16:02.270
But what about the
problem of paints?

16:02.270 --> 16:04.780
Normal paints won’t
stick to soft vinyl.

16:04.780 --> 16:08.210
We’ll market special paints through the
same sales channels as the kits.

16:08.210 --> 16:10.440
We’ll not only sell the product…

16:10.440 --> 16:12.810
…but also the whole culture
the product possesses!

16:12.810 --> 16:14.940
We can have consultants at all
our stores to demonstrate…

16:14.940 --> 16:18.510
…new techniques to customers.

16:18.510 --> 16:23.090
All right. I’ll talk with group leaders.
You keep working on it.

16:24.000 --> 16:27.630
There’s no doubt that soft vinyl kits
will be a revolutionary product!

16:27.630 --> 16:30.430
If they aren’t, there’ll be
no point in doing them!

16:33.660 --> 16:36.140
September 7, that same year.

16:39.420 --> 16:41.210
People’s Republic of China

16:41.640 --> 16:45.580
“GP China Factory”

16:50.260 --> 16:53.820
Mr. President! Mr. President!
A package! For you, from Japan.

16:54.430 --> 16:56.530
Ah, I’ve been waiting for this.

16:56.530 --> 16:59.900
They must’ve completed the designs
for the new product.

17:00.430 --> 17:02.120
This is it!

17:02.120 --> 17:06.190
The original character I’ve
been searching for!

17:06.190 --> 17:08.430
“Märchen”: German for “fairy tale.”

17:06.190 --> 17:08.430
I’ll call her “Märchen Doll Maki!”

17:08.430 --> 17:14.450
She’s going to be the one who saves
Japan’s garage kit industry!

17:14.450 --> 17:15.780
You see something?

17:22.000 --> 17:24.060
Akasaka, Minato District, Tokyo

17:25.130 --> 17:28.230
This way, please.

17:28.230 --> 17:32.490
Your guest has arrived.

17:35.700 --> 17:38.780
I’ve been expecting you,
Director Tanaka.

17:40.170 --> 17:42.640
You… You’re…

17:43.580 --> 17:55.110
1988/07/23:
Self-defense forces submarine “Nadashio”
and fishing boat “Dai-Ichi Fuji Maru”
collide and sink in Yokosuka Bay.
30 fatalities.

17:57.350 --> 18:04.190
Portrait of an Otaku

18:07.730 --> 18:14.760
Shon Hernandez
(pseudonym)
Born 1964.
Missionary.
Years as an otaku: 6.

18:23.080 --> 18:29.810
I think I was about 21 when I first
developed an interest in Japan.

18:29.810 --> 18:35.620
It all started when my friend from
Portugal showed me a book…

18:35.620 --> 18:38.490
…I think “Animec” was its name.

18:38.490 --> 18:44.460
When I saw it, I remember experiencing
a severe culture shock.

18:44.460 --> 18:47.590
That was when I began to
want to come to Japan…

18:47.590 --> 18:50.860
…to learn about, and preach about this
beautiful and wonderful thing…

18:50.860 --> 18:53.670
…to the unknowing people
of the world.

18:53.670 --> 18:57.240
I felt that God had commanded
me to do this work.

18:57.240 --> 18:59.740
How did you feel when you
first came to Japan?

18:59.740 --> 19:01.780
Fantastic!

19:01.780 --> 19:03.780
The reason I live in a place
like this is to cut down…

19:03.780 --> 19:06.510
…on food and living expenses, so I
can save and use all my money…

19:06.510 --> 19:07.850
…including what I amassed
before coming to Japan…

19:07.850 --> 19:10.010
…by selling all my past possessions…

19:10.010 --> 19:15.190
…to buy and collect Japan’s splendid
books and other merchandise.

19:15.190 --> 19:18.260
Unlike American cartoons
and comics…

19:18.260 --> 19:21.760
…Japanese anime has taken a totally
different evolutionary path.

19:21.760 --> 19:25.770
The beauty of the characters, the
power of the Gundam robot…

19:25.770 --> 19:29.000
It is all something to be admired.

19:29.000 --> 19:33.230
I am therefore willing to devote
my life to this culture.

19:33.610 --> 19:36.110
Do you have a character that
you particularly like?

19:39.520 --> 19:43.020
Well, I was really surprised that
what Americans call “Satan”…

19:43.020 --> 19:45.990
…which, I understand, is
called “oni” in Japan…

19:45.990 --> 19:50.260
…was turned from a scary concept
into a cute girl called “Lum.”

19:50.260 --> 19:52.920
That has to be the best character.

19:52.920 --> 19:58.440
Like they say, it’s the devilish nature of
women that makes them fascinating.

19:58.440 --> 20:02.800
They labeled a beautiful woman
with a keyword like “oni”…

20:02.800 --> 20:08.510
…and used it to give the fans a modern
take on traditional Japanese stories.

20:08.510 --> 20:13.310
I am always fascinated by these
clever Japanese techniques.

20:13.310 --> 20:19.690
Can you imagine an American drawing
Devil Girl or Kerberos Shoujo?

20:19.690 --> 20:22.200
Are you happy?

20:22.200 --> 20:24.160
No, I am not.

20:24.160 --> 20:29.570
Why couldn’t I have been born
in this golden land?

20:29.570 --> 20:32.000
Without regard to their
nationality, race…

20:33.070 --> 20:35.740
…or status as illegal workers
on tourist visas…

20:35.740 --> 20:39.470
…we interviewed some
foreigners at random.

20:39.470 --> 20:41.570
The results are shown here.

20:41.570 --> 20:44.050
Surprisingly, more than
one out of two…

20:44.050 --> 20:46.050
…or over 50% of the people
we interviewed…

20:46.050 --> 20:50.990
…had, in some form, been baptized
into the otaku subculture in Japan.

20:50.990 --> 20:58.490
And over half of these people either
”like” or are “not opposed” to it.

20:58.490 --> 21:04.020
Recently in America, there has been a
boom in “Japanimation” and manga.

21:04.020 --> 21:08.350
How will the otaku culture affect
future Japan–USA relations?

21:12.760 --> 21:14.710
Banner: “To Celebrate China
Factory Completion!”

21:14.710 --> 21:17.940
Sign: “Japan–China Friendship Party”

21:16.470 --> 21:20.130
Cheers!

21:20.260 --> 21:23.480
Completion of the GP China Factory

21:32.920 --> 21:34.320
It’s me…

21:34.320 --> 21:37.310
What?! An emergency
board meeting?!

21:52.400 --> 21:54.440
Sign: “Conference Room Two”

21:58.150 --> 22:01.210
You’re… Mr. Kanda, of Tozai Bank!

22:01.210 --> 22:05.670
Right on schedule. Shall we begin?

22:06.290 --> 22:10.620
We’ll start by introducing a motion
for President Kubo’s dismissal.

22:10.620 --> 22:13.700
All in favor, raise your hand.

22:13.700 --> 22:15.310
Wh-What?!

22:15.310 --> 22:19.100
By majority vote, the motion
is seconded.

22:19.670 --> 22:21.810
Tanaka! What’s the
meaning of this?!

22:21.810 --> 22:25.910
Moving on, all those in favor of
President Kubo’s dismissal…

22:25.910 --> 22:28.340
…please raise your hands.

22:31.980 --> 22:34.160
<i>This is crazy.</i>

22:34.690 --> 22:38.370
By majority vote, President
Kubo has been dismissed.

22:38.370 --> 22:39.620
Hold on a minute!

22:39.620 --> 22:40.920
What do you think you’re doing?!

22:40.920 --> 22:42.790
Grand Prix is my company!

22:42.790 --> 22:45.090
Don’t you get it?

22:45.090 --> 22:50.260
You brought this on yourself with
that selfish attitude of yours.

22:52.400 --> 22:54.200
You bastard!

22:54.200 --> 22:58.600
To continue, allow me to introduce
the new president…

23:02.380 --> 23:05.000
Ueno… Yoshiko?!

23:08.690 --> 23:10.490
Ueno is my maiden name.

23:10.490 --> 23:12.850
Now she’s Kanda Yoshiko…

23:12.850 --> 23:17.200
In other words, she’s my wife.

23:19.530 --> 23:22.290
You won’t get away with this…

23:22.290 --> 23:24.890
No matter what anyone says,
I won’t let you get away with it!

23:24.890 --> 23:28.590
Guys! What’s the matter with you?
Why are you falling for this ploy?

23:38.020 --> 23:39.210
G-Guys…

23:39.210 --> 23:41.910
Mr. Kubo…

23:42.550 --> 23:45.650
We have arranged a new
posting for you.

23:46.390 --> 23:51.610
As of today, you’ll be going
to the GP Kichijoji branch.

23:52.230 --> 23:54.760
Go and dream your dreams there…

24:01.570 --> 24:08.810
1989/01/08:
The Showa Emperor dies,
ushering in the Heisei era.

24:11.640 --> 24:16.770
Portrait of an Otaku

24:19.480 --> 24:26.950
Akabori Osamu
(pseudonym)
Born 1972.
Culinary school
student.
Years as an otaku: 9.

24:31.650 --> 24:34.760
See… this is called
”Cybernetic High School.”

24:34.760 --> 24:37.590
See, see? Isn’t Hiroko cute?

24:37.590 --> 24:41.070
Well, basically, it’s a quiz game.

24:41.070 --> 24:44.570
It’s a quiz with… hold on a sec.

24:44.570 --> 24:48.610
Yeah, mostly it covers anime
and special effects films.

24:48.610 --> 24:53.380
Oh… Damn, hold on…

24:53.380 --> 24:55.220
Do you ever admire the blue sky?

24:56.220 --> 25:03.270
Yes, well, I don’t go outside much.
I don’t like to sweat and stuff.

25:03.270 --> 25:05.270
How do you take care of
your sexual needs?

25:05.270 --> 25:08.590
Well, I like computer games.

25:08.590 --> 25:11.000
It’s good when the girls are cute.

25:11.000 --> 25:13.610
Err… Don’t you think Hiroko’s cute?

25:13.610 --> 25:16.360
There’s been a lot of adult
stuff lately, you see…

25:16.360 --> 25:21.500
…and I’m a fan of all that stuff.

25:21.500 --> 25:23.700
See… See…

25:23.700 --> 25:26.420
Is she cute… or what?

25:26.980 --> 25:29.450
Ah, hold on a sec…

25:32.390 --> 25:36.050
Look, look, look…

25:36.050 --> 25:41.470
Well? Do you like her? I really
like her like this, you see…

25:42.000 --> 25:51.320
Err, well… Uh, um… uh…

25:51.320 --> 25:53.690
Don’t you think what you’re
doing is indecent?

25:53.690 --> 26:01.080
Ah… Um… I guess I do,
now that you mention it.

26:01.080 --> 26:04.000
But I don’t worry about it.

26:04.000 --> 26:06.990
Have you ever thought about
having sex with a real girl?

26:08.060 --> 26:14.050
Well, uh, you know…

26:14.050 --> 26:17.360
Don’t you think Hiroko is pretty?

26:18.370 --> 26:21.060
One of the noticeable traits
of the otaku is that…

26:21.060 --> 26:24.260
…they often talk to themselves.

26:24.260 --> 26:29.280
This graph clearly shows that
this is indeed the case.

26:29.280 --> 26:32.020
The most important thing
is their own world…

26:32.020 --> 26:34.750
…a world within the heart, which
no one else can intrude upon.

26:34.750 --> 26:38.180
There, even today, otaku
will converse alone.

27:19.560 --> 27:22.550
<i>Go and dream your</i>
<i>dreams there…</i>

27:24.500 --> 27:26.960
Goddammit!

27:33.300 --> 27:35.770
Maybe I should just go home…

27:37.000 --> 27:40.830
“GP Announces New Product”

27:44.420 --> 27:47.090
“Explosive Popularity Beginning
With Pre-Orders”

27:47.090 --> 27:50.480
“Garage Kit Boom Re-lgnites”
”Approved at Soviet Conference”

28:07.510 --> 28:12.000
Damn, damn! So careless
of me to come this way…

28:13.140 --> 28:14.460
Tanaka!

28:21.850 --> 28:25.010
Forgive me, Kubo! I was a fool!

28:25.520 --> 28:27.930
What in the world happened
to you, Tanaka?

28:27.930 --> 28:31.790
I was being used by the bank guys.

28:31.790 --> 28:34.900
As soon as you were out of the picture,
they threw me out like trash.

28:34.900 --> 28:39.860
I was framed for embezzlement,
then fired without any severance pay.

28:40.240 --> 28:44.200
Now I’m living hand to mouth,
one day at a time.

28:44.200 --> 28:47.900
And yet, old habits
are hard to break…

28:47.900 --> 28:51.300
…and so, before I knew it,
here I am at this store.

28:53.580 --> 28:57.790
Tanaka… why don’t we start
from scratch, all over again.

28:58.490 --> 29:02.020
Kubo! You mean you’ll
forgive me?

29:02.330 --> 29:04.970
What are you talking
about, Tanaka?

29:06.960 --> 29:09.890
Are we not otaku?

29:11.570 --> 29:13.930
As otaku, we’ll…

29:13.930 --> 29:17.970
…keep living on!

29:19.840 --> 29:28.410
1991/01/01:
The 23 districts of Tokyo
increase the telephone prefix
length from 3 to 4 digits.

29:31.640 --> 29:37.210
Portrait of an Otaku

29:40.490 --> 29:51.420
Murayama Akira
(pseudonym)
Born 1964.
Unemployed.
Years as an otaku: 10.
Years as a cel thief: 6.

29:54.140 --> 29:55.980
What is a cel thief?

30:01.240 --> 30:03.940
Like the name says, it’s
a thief who steals cels.

30:03.140 --> 30:10.920
Voice has been altered at the subject’s
request. Please bear with us.

30:03.940 --> 30:08.680
I also steal designs and original art,
but mostly, I steal cels.

30:08.680 --> 30:10.080
Yeah, it’s really easy.

30:10.080 --> 30:13.780
Animation companies are usually
open 24 hours a day…

30:13.780 --> 30:17.710
…so you can just walk in, say
”good morning,” and score.

30:17.710 --> 30:19.150
No one at the companies
remembers the faces…

30:19.150 --> 30:21.520
…of all the subcontractors’
production-advancement people…

30:21.520 --> 30:23.720
…so there’s no worry at all
about being suspected.

30:23.720 --> 30:31.030
Well… I sell the goods within my own
in-group or at secret swap meets.

30:31.030 --> 30:32.830
I make copies of the design sheets…

30:32.830 --> 30:35.680
…and sell them at Comiket
for some serious money.

30:35.680 --> 30:37.520
Do you consider yourself a criminal?

30:41.750 --> 30:45.020
Well, there are guys who, even
knowing that they’re stolen…

30:45.020 --> 30:49.450
…buy and hoard cels like
they’re treasures.

30:49.450 --> 30:52.790
Since that’s the way the world
works, I say, “so be it.”

30:52.790 --> 30:55.590
And there’s also a code in stealing.

30:55.590 --> 30:59.630
I make it a point never to steal cels
that haven’t been shot yet.

30:59.630 --> 31:03.500
If I did that, it would slow the production
of animation that I want to see.

31:03.500 --> 31:06.870
I’d never do that.

31:08.140 --> 31:13.260
Naturally, I don’t plan to quit.
This is my livelihood.

31:13.260 --> 31:17.510
I think as long as there’s a demand,
there’ll be a supply. Definitely.

31:21.460 --> 31:24.980
Not all otaku are just.

31:24.980 --> 31:27.020
If there’s an otaku who’ll
follow his dreams…

31:27.020 --> 31:31.260
…there’s an otaku who’ll
feed on those dreams.

31:31.260 --> 31:35.490
Unfortunately, this graph shows
that this is all too true.

31:35.490 --> 31:38.900
When a desire exists, people
will do anything to satisfy it.

31:38.900 --> 31:42.440
This emotion is strongest
in an otaku.

31:42.440 --> 31:48.870
Even acts such as committing
crimes become mere trifles.

31:53.780 --> 31:55.140
Video software?

31:55.140 --> 31:58.850
Right! We’ll make our own
videos and sell them.

31:59.490 --> 32:02.810
If we make them original,
the copyright’s ours.

32:03.760 --> 32:08.450
Then we can sell books, kits,
or whatever we want.

32:08.450 --> 32:11.470
But Tanaka, how are we
going to do this?

32:11.470 --> 32:14.780
It costs hundreds of millions
of yen to make a movie.

32:15.710 --> 32:18.240
I have an 8mm camera.

32:18.240 --> 32:19.770
Eight-millimeter?!

32:19.770 --> 32:22.460
Tanaka, do you think we can make
anything worth selling with that?

32:23.750 --> 32:26.670
Kubo, otaku are not ordinary!

32:26.670 --> 32:28.070
Of course!

32:28.070 --> 32:31.630
If we were able to sell garage kits,
then why not garage videos?

32:31.630 --> 32:33.110
That’s right!

32:33.110 --> 32:36.060
We’ll start off with some
sure-fire anime.

32:36.060 --> 32:39.130
We can use home VCRs
to make the copies.

32:39.130 --> 32:41.530
Then, we’ll do mail order
through the magazines…

32:41.530 --> 32:43.860
…and at screenings, we’ll
sell merchandise.

32:43.860 --> 32:44.730
How about it, Kubo?

32:44.730 --> 32:47.060
Once again, all that lies beneath
the heavens will be ours!

32:47.060 --> 32:48.600
Hold on, Tanaka!

32:48.600 --> 32:50.140
Who’s going to do it?

32:50.140 --> 32:52.100
Neither one of us can draw!

32:52.910 --> 32:58.710
Have no fear. Anticipating this,
I secretly contacted someone…

32:59.790 --> 33:01.540
Oh! You’re…

33:01.540 --> 33:03.210
…Miss Fukuhara!

33:03.210 --> 33:05.320
But why you?

33:05.320 --> 33:08.580
Even though she was on the
enemy’s side for a while…

33:08.580 --> 33:12.020
…in her heart, she
felt closer to us.

33:12.020 --> 33:15.130
Umm, if I’m a bother, I’ll…

33:15.130 --> 33:17.630
No, of course you’re not!

33:17.630 --> 33:19.890
With you on our side, we’ll have
the strength of a thousand!

33:19.890 --> 33:21.330
I’m counting on you!

33:21.330 --> 33:23.440
Sure!

33:23.440 --> 33:24.400
Miss Fukuhara…

33:24.400 --> 33:25.500
Yes?

33:25.500 --> 33:26.560
Did you bring “it?”

33:26.560 --> 33:28.950
Yes!

33:28.950 --> 33:30.840
Please take a look at this.

33:37.190 --> 33:39.750
This is Märchen Doll Maki, isn’t it?!

33:39.750 --> 33:41.160
Wrong!

33:41.160 --> 33:43.120
You’re losing it, Kubo!

33:43.120 --> 33:44.020
But…

33:44.020 --> 33:46.580
She may look similar, but
she’s a little bit different.

33:47.500 --> 33:49.200
Tanaka…

33:49.200 --> 33:52.890
The same person drew it, so
naturally it looks similar.

33:52.890 --> 33:55.720
But this time, there’s a story
that goes along with it.

33:56.470 --> 33:59.640
The main character, Satsuki, is a quiet
girl who’s always being picked on.

33:59.640 --> 34:03.800
But when she gets hold
of her baton… Wow…

34:03.800 --> 34:06.880
…she changes into the
Super Idol, Misty May!

34:06.880 --> 34:11.930
Best of all, she lacks Märchen Doll
Maki’s greatest weakness!

34:11.930 --> 34:15.320
Maki’s weakness? What’s that?

34:16.830 --> 34:18.330
Mascots!

34:19.370 --> 34:21.830
Correct! Stuff for girls
always has mascots…

34:21.830 --> 34:25.860
…as they are cute comic relief
and important co-stars.

34:25.860 --> 34:26.960
Therefore, we have…

34:26.960 --> 34:29.580
Posi-King and Nega-King!

34:30.770 --> 34:33.300
Posi-King? Nega-King?

34:36.110 --> 34:38.370
I like it! I like it!

34:38.370 --> 34:40.050
Okay, I’ve got it!

34:40.050 --> 34:42.280
We’ll form a new company
right away!

34:42.280 --> 34:45.010
The company’s name should
suggest magnitude…

34:45.010 --> 34:47.600
Yes! How about “Giant X?!”

34:47.600 --> 34:49.660
Or “GX” for short!

36:02.400 --> 36:05.590
“Animage Magazine”

36:08.820 --> 36:12.410
“Animedia Magazine”

36:13.500 --> 36:15.160
First Screening

36:22.750 --> 36:23.980
Action!

36:35.300 --> 36:38.200
(Misty May videos all rented out.)

36:38.200 --> 36:41.680
“GX: Conquering the World
With Garage Video?!”

36:44.900 --> 36:47.810
Tokyo Dome

37:21.300 --> 37:24.100
“GX Buys Out Garage
Kit Manufacturer GP”

37:24.100 --> 37:27.010
“Gorby Serious About
Garage Kit Industry”

37:27.010 --> 37:29.080
“GP Exporting Video to Communist Bloc”
”Three Animation Titles Initially”

37:29.080 --> 37:30.810
“Big Hit in Moscow! Percentage
of Video Owners on Rise”
”Longer Lines Than for Black Bread!”
”GX Expands Product Line”

37:30.810 --> 37:33.820
Secretary of State Baker
Washington, this morning.

37:31.290 --> 37:34.500
“Super-301 Sanctions Against
GP and GX Products”

37:34.500 --> 37:37.100
“The Japan That Can Say ‘Oh No!’
by Kubo Ken and Tanaka Naeo”
”Smash Bestseller”
”Must-Read for Businessmen”

37:47.200 --> 37:51.790
April 1st

37:52.340 --> 37:56.440
Tokyo Otakuland

38:02.020 --> 38:04.020
We’ve done it, Kubo!

38:04.020 --> 38:06.610
Even our mansions in Beverly
Hills are completed!

38:06.610 --> 38:10.560
I’ve done it! Finally… Finally,
I have achieved my dream.

38:10.560 --> 38:12.820
I’m the otaku of otaku…

38:13.960 --> 38:16.430
…Otaking!

38:18.800 --> 38:21.800
And it is I…

38:21.800 --> 38:24.470
…who has become the
Terrible Great King!

38:30.480 --> 38:38.750
1999/09/13:
An accidental explosion occurs at the
Nuclear Waste Disposal Facility
on the far side of the Moon.

38:41.530 --> 38:48.770
Portrait of an Otaku

38:49.440 --> 38:58.740
Uesaka Hidehiko
(pseudonym)
A true otaku, whom
our staff discovered
as a result of seven
weeks of ongoing
investigation.

38:58.740 --> 39:00.050
Hold it. There he goes…

39:00.050 --> 39:01.490
Okay.

39:01.490 --> 39:04.590
Late on the night of June 21, 1991…

39:01.490 --> 39:03.150
Okay.

39:03.150 --> 39:05.060
He’s passing alongside the car now.

39:04.590 --> 39:08.380
…he was apparently returning
from a cel smugglers’ meet.

39:05.060 --> 39:07.520
Roger, we’ll follow.

39:08.380 --> 39:16.230
We finally had a chance to approach him.

39:11.000 --> 39:13.030
Okay, go! Go! Go!

39:13.030 --> 39:15.470
You idiot, slow down!

39:17.800 --> 39:20.270
Easy, easy…

39:24.880 --> 39:28.420
Go slowly… Okay, he hasn’t noticed.

39:30.750 --> 39:35.210
Okay, okay, stop him! Stop him!

39:44.230 --> 39:45.900
Excuse me, hold on a second!

39:45.900 --> 39:47.190
Excuse me! Just a moment.

39:47.190 --> 39:48.400
What! What do you want?!

39:48.400 --> 39:50.390
A moment of your time, please.

39:50.390 --> 39:53.040
We’d like to talk to
you for a second.

39:53.040 --> 39:53.900
What?! What’s this?!

39:53.900 --> 39:55.670
Can we talk with you a second?

39:55.670 --> 39:56.640
What? Is this TV?

39:56.640 --> 39:58.100
Yes. We’d like to ask you something…

39:58.100 --> 39:58.800
What are you doing?!

39:58.800 --> 40:01.580
Sorry! Yes! Only for a moment, Okay?

40:01.580 --> 40:06.280
What is this, an interview?

40:06.280 --> 40:09.180
It’s about that bag… What’s in it?

40:09.180 --> 40:11.630
What?! What difference does it make?
It’s none of your business!

40:11.630 --> 40:13.250
Please, just a few questions…

40:13.250 --> 40:15.300
Get out of here!

40:15.300 --> 40:16.330
You okay?

40:16.330 --> 40:18.030
Go, go! I’m okay!

40:26.760 --> 40:29.700
36 years later.

40:40.200 --> 40:43.700
Japan

40:44.300 --> 40:48.880
Kanto Bay

40:57.600 --> 41:02.100
Old Tokyo, Edogawa District Vicinity

41:07.600 --> 41:12.210
What on Earth is down here,
at the bottom of the sea?

41:08.000 --> 41:12.640
Urayasu City, Chiba Prefecture

41:12.210 --> 41:14.610
Our youth.

41:29.260 --> 41:35.280
It all started here, didn’t it, Kubo?

41:36.470 --> 41:42.810
Yes. From the time I decided to
build this place, 40 years ago…

41:42.810 --> 41:47.770
Otakuland… Oh, it’s
all so dear to me…

42:09.840 --> 42:12.290
What exactly is going on here?

42:12.290 --> 42:14.670
Are we dreaming?!

42:27.310 --> 42:28.480
You…

42:28.480 --> 42:33.850
Iiyama! Miyoshi! Hino!
Tsurumaki! Sato!

42:33.850 --> 42:36.990
Kubo, you don’t need helmets here.

42:53.940 --> 42:56.550
Blast off!

43:13.400 --> 43:15.560
Where do we go from here?

43:15.560 --> 43:17.570
There can only be one place, Tanaka.

43:30.150 --> 43:32.750
The stars… are our destination!

43:33.980 --> 43:36.780
We’ll boldly go, to seek out…
the planet of the otaku!

44:10.320 --> 44:19.120
2035/05/15:
Launch of the “Eltrium,”
a 5th-generation space battleship.

44:40.250 --> 44:48.120
<i>Near Makuhari, where the snow is</i>
<i>falling, I walk around with you.</i>

44:48.120 --> 44:55.800
<i>Even the cold ocean</i>
<i>breeze feels gentle.</i>

44:55.800 --> 45:03.340
<i>You, with your dazzling smile, your</i>
<i>Char cosplay is wonderful too.</i>

45:03.340 --> 45:13.520
<i>Hold me tight, just like Lalah.</i>

45:13.520 --> 45:17.190
<i>I love you.</i>

45:17.190 --> 45:21.060
<i>I love you too, so much.</i>

45:21.060 --> 45:24.830
<i>Let’s spend the night in line.</i>

45:24.830 --> 45:28.400
<i>Anime is the best.</i>

45:28.400 --> 45:32.330
<i>It’s the shining memory</i>
<i>of our youth.</i>

45:32.330 --> 45:35.840
<i>We’ll always be together.</i>

45:35.840 --> 45:39.710
<i>An endless hope that connects</i>
<i>even our dreams together.</i>

45:39.710 --> 45:43.380
<i>We’ll set our sights on working</i>
<i>in the industry.</i>

45:43.380 --> 45:51.090
<i>Ah, a fulfilling life!</i>

45:51.090 --> 45:58.730
<i>The lost way of otaku…</i>
